# shir_nodes/string_affix.node — expression-level semantic IR node.
# Prefix/suffix test (lowered from `[[ $x == P* ]]`, `case P*)`).
#
# Backends: Perl index()==0, JS startsWith/endsWith, C strncmp, Rust starts_with
# Fallback: sh2.stringAffix(text, pattern, prefix)

node StringAffix
tag "StringAffix"
kind expr
# storage_class str_bounded
field text: expr
field pattern: expr
field prefix: bool
# RESOLVED — the value is "the pattern sits at the enabled END of the text": a prefix test
# for `prefix: true`, a suffix test for `prefix: false`. TextOps#string_affix_sound carries
# the whole contract (the bare take/drop equations are just the definition):
#  * THE READING: `has_affix t p True ⟷ ∃r. t = p @ r` and
#    `has_affix t p False ⟷ ∃r. t = r @ p` — this is what `[[ $x == P* ]]` and `case P*)`
#    mean, and what `startsWith`/`endsWith`/`strncmp` compute;
#  * ENDS: the empty pattern is an affix of everything on both sides (which is why
#    `${s#}` with an empty literal pattern is the identity), the empty TEXT admits only the
#    empty pattern, and a pattern longer than the text is never an affix;
#  * BOUNDED: a prefix match implies `length p ≤ length t`, so the take/drop reads are
#    always in range.
# NON-VACUITY: TextOps#string_affix_witness — `a` prefixes `ab`, `b` suffixes it, each is
# NOT an affix at the other end, the empty pattern affixes the empty text, and the whole
# text is a prefix of itself.
# REFUTED: matching anywhere (Perl `index() != -1` instead of `== 0`, the classic slip —
# TextOps#anywhere_is_not_the_affix) and a length-only test
# (TextOps#length_alone_is_not_the_affix).
# NOT PROMISED (outside this model): the pattern is a LITERAL byte string, not a glob; the
# shell pattern language (`*`, `?`, `[...]`, extglob) and the Perl trailing-arm empty-pattern
# divergence (proved separately as a renderer gap in this theory) are out of model.
spec: TextOps#string_affix_sound
